# Human factors and high-altitude physiology: PHAK chapters 2 and 17; AIM chapter 8; Risk Management
# Handbook FAA-H-8083-2A; FAA medication guidance for pilots; 14 CFR 91.3. ACS I.H and VIII.
ITEMS = [
    # ---------- Practice Test 1 ----------
    S(1, 2, "Time of useful consciousness", "After a sudden loss of pressurization at 25,000 feet, about how long is the average time of useful consciousness for a seated pilot?",
      ["3 to 5 minutes", "20 to 30 minutes", "30 to 60 seconds"],
      "The PHAK (chapter 17) gives an average time of useful consciousness of about 3 to 5 minutes at 25,000 feet, 1 to 2 minutes at 30,000 and 30 to 60 seconds at 35,000. Don oxygen at once."),
    S(1, 2, "Pilot in command authority", "Your employer tells you to take a charter flight in an airplane with an inoperative instrument required for the flight. Who has final authority over whether the flight goes?",
      ["You, as pilot in command", "The employer, as operator of the airplane", "The customer who chartered the flight"],
      "Under 14 CFR 91.3 the pilot in command is directly responsible for, and is the final authority as to, the operation of the aircraft. Pressure from an employer or customer does not change that."),
    S(1, 2, "Stagnant hypoxia", "Which type of hypoxia can high G loads in steep maneuvers cause?",
      ["Stagnant hypoxia, from blood pooling away from the brain", "Hypemic hypoxia, from a drop in red blood cells", "Histotoxic hypoxia, from cells unable to use oxygen"],
      "Stagnant hypoxia means oxygen-rich blood is not flowing to the tissues. Pulling excessive Gs can pool blood in the lower body (PHAK chapter 17)."),
    S(1, 3, "Decompression sickness", "After a long flight at 25,000 feet in an unpressurized airplane, you have deep pain in your knees and elbows. What is the likely cause, and the right response?",
      ["Decompression sickness: use 100 percent oxygen, descend and get medical help", "Hyperventilation: breathe into a bag and continue the flight", "Dehydration: drink water and climb to cooler air"],
      "Nitrogen coming out of solution in the body causes decompression sickness, the bends, with joint pain. Breathe 100 percent oxygen, descend as low as practical and seek medical care; a hyperbaric chamber may be needed (PHAK chapter 17)."),
    # ---------- Practice Test 2 ----------
    S(2, 3, "Medications", "FAA guidance says to wait at least five times the maximum dosing interval after a sedating over-the-counter medicine. For a medicine taken every 6 hours, how long is that?",
      ["30 hours", "12 hours", "8 hours"],
      "Five times the 6-hour dosing interval is 30 hours. The FAA's guidance on medications uses this rule for drugs that can impair performance."),
    S(2, 2, "Ear and sinus block", "You have a head cold. When is a painful ear or sinus block most likely?",
      ["During descent, when outside pressure rises and cannot equalize", "During climb, when outside pressure falls", "In cruise, after the pressure has equalized"],
      "On descent the rising outside pressure must push air into the middle ear and sinuses. A cold can block the passages, causing pain. Do not fly with a cold that blocks them (PHAK chapter 17)."),
    S(2, 2, "Rapid decompression", "The cabin suddenly fills with mist and there is a loud rush of air at FL230. What is your first action?",
      ["Put on your oxygen mask", "Check the outflow valve setting", "Reduce power and turn back"],
      "Fog, noise and a temperature drop are signs of rapid decompression. Time of useful consciousness is short, so don oxygen first, then begin an emergency descent (ACS VIII.B)."),
    S(2, 2, "Histotoxic hypoxia", "Why does alcohol cause hypoxia even when the blood carries enough oxygen?",
      ["It interferes with the cells' ability to use oxygen", "It lowers the number of red blood cells", "It reduces the oxygen pressure in the lungs"],
      "Alcohol and some drugs cause histotoxic hypoxia: oxygen reaches the tissues but the cells cannot use it properly (PHAK chapter 17)."),
    # ---------- Drill only ----------
    S(0, 2, "Hypoxic hypoxia", "What causes hypoxic hypoxia at altitude?",
      ["The lower partial pressure of oxygen in the thinner air", "A lower percentage of oxygen in the air above 10,000 feet", "Carbon monoxide from the engine exhaust"],
      "The air is still about 21 percent oxygen at altitude, but its lower pressure pushes less oxygen into the blood (PHAK chapter 17)."),
    S(0, 2, "Fatigue", "What is the difference between acute and chronic fatigue?",
      ["Acute fatigue follows short-term strain and passes with rest; chronic fatigue builds up and needs longer recovery", "Acute fatigue lasts weeks; chronic fatigue passes with one good night's sleep", "They are the same thing with different names"],
      "Acute fatigue comes from a day's physical or mental strain and goes away with adequate rest. Chronic fatigue builds over a long period and may need extended rest or medical help (PHAK chapter 17)."),
    S(0, 2, "Dehydration", "How can a pilot avoid dehydration on a long summer flight?",
      ["Drink water regularly before you feel thirsty", "Wait until you feel thirsty, then drink a large amount", "Drink coffee to stay alert"],
      "Thirst is a late sign of dehydration, and caffeine adds to fluid loss. Drink water steadily through the day (PHAK chapter 17)."),
    S(0, 2, "Operational pitfalls", "A commercial pilot keeps descending under lowering clouds to stay VFR and reach a customer's destination. What is this pitfall called?",
      ["Scud running", "Loss of positional awareness", "Descent below the minimum safe altitude in IMC"],
      "Scud running, pushing on beneath lowering ceilings, is one of the operational pitfalls in the PHAK (chapter 2). It often ends in controlled flight into terrain."),
    S(0, 2, "Landing illusions", "Approaching over featureless terrain such as water or snow, what illusion may you have and what may you do?",
      ["You feel higher than you are and fly a lower approach", "You feel lower than you are and fly a higher approach", "You feel slower than you are and add speed"],
      "Without ground detail the eye judges height poorly. The usual result is the illusion of being higher than actual and a lower-than-normal approach (AIM 8-1-5)."),
    S(0, 2, "Landing illusions", "What illusion can atmospheric haze cause on approach?",
      ["The runway seems farther away than it is", "The runway seems closer than it is", "The runway seems to slope uphill"],
      "Haze makes the runway appear farther away, so pilots tend to fly a low approach. Very clear air can make it seem closer (AIM 8-1-5)."),
    S(0, 2, "Ground lighting illusions", "At night, a line of lights along a straight road can lead you to what error?",
      ["Mistaking the road lights for runway lights", "Thinking you are climbing when you are level", "Believing the airport beacon is a star"],
      "Lights along a straight road or on moving trains can be mistaken for runway and approach lights (AIM 8-1-5)."),
    S(0, 2, "Time of useful consciousness", "About what is the time of useful consciousness at 35,000 feet?",
      ["30 to 60 seconds", "3 to 5 minutes", "10 to 15 minutes"],
      "The PHAK (chapter 17) lists 30 to 60 seconds at 35,000 feet. That is why a pilot above FL350 must wear an oxygen mask in many cases (14 CFR 91.211(b))."),
    S(0, 2, "Hypoxia training", "What is a good way to learn your own hypoxia symptoms before flying high?",
      ["Take FAA physiology training with an altitude chamber or hypoxia trainer", "Fly briefly above 18,000 feet without oxygen and note what happens", "Read the symptoms in the airplane's manual"],
      "Symptoms differ from person to person. The FAA's Civil Aerospace Medical Institute offers physiology training where pilots can safely experience hypoxia (PHAK chapter 17)."),
    S(0, 2, "Trapped gas", "What happens to gas trapped in the stomach and intestines as an unpressurized airplane climbs?",
      ["It expands and can cause pain", "It contracts and can cause nausea", "It dissolves into the blood"],
      "As outside pressure falls, trapped gas expands. Avoid gas-forming foods before high-altitude flights (PHAK chapter 17)."),
    S(0, 2, "Commercial pressures", "A paying passenger urges you to leave right away despite a thunderstorm moving toward the airport. What is the best response?",
      ["Explain the risk and wait until the storm has passed", "Depart at once to keep the customer satisfied", "Depart and plan to outrun the storm at low altitude"],
      "Passenger pressure is a classic external pressure. Risk management means you decide on the facts, not the schedule, and a thunderstorm near the airport brings wind shear and microburst risk (FAA-H-8083-2A)."),
]
